\title[Pooling geometry and capacity--fade duality]{Pooling Geometry, the Sharp
Seed-Imbalance Law, and Capacity--Fade Duality for Correlation Ladders}

\author{Aristotle}
\date{25 August 2026}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
We develop an exact geometric theory of \emph{pooled} correlation --- the number
an experiment reports when it concatenates several independent blocks (seeds)
and correlates the resulting long vectors --- and use it to analyse a decaying
ladder of correlation readings. Pooling never inflates a reading
($\rpool \le \max_k \rho_k$), the reverse inequality fails strictly (two blocks
each reading $1$ pool to $3/\sqrt{10}$), and pooling is an energy-weighted
average exactly on balanced families. If the per-block norm ratios lie in a
window $[\alpha,\beta]$ with $\alpha>0$ and every block reads at least $\rho$,
then $\rpool \ge \rho \cdot 2\sqrt{\alpha\beta}/(\alpha+\beta)$; this Kantorovich
constant is attained, its extremiser is unique as a distribution, a single
interior seed forces strict inequality, and a quantitative stability estimate
bounds the distance to the extremiser by the slack. From Gram positivity alone,
$(a-b)^2 \le 2(1-c)$, so any measured advantage certifies decorrelation; this is
exactly the AM--GM relaxation of the ellipse certificate
$c \le ab + \sqrt{(1-a^2)(1-b^2)}$. Finally, defining the capacity of a reading
as $\cpc(\rho) = \lfloor 1/\rho^2\rfloor$, a persistent multiplicative fade
drives the capacity above every level, and conversely a capacity ceiling $K$ is
exactly a positive floor $\rho_N^2 > 1/(K+1)$ at every rung: ``floor'' and
``bounded capacity'' are the same hypothesis. We also solve the inverse pooling
problem, pinning a two-sided window for the unreported per-seed readings from
the pooled value and the imbalance window.
\end{abstract}

