\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Three ideas carry the development. \emph{Concatenation is not averaging}, and
the gap is exactly the spread of the ratio profile, measured exactly by the
Kantorovich constant; because that constant is sharp it can be used
adversarially, to refute artefact explanations rather than merely tolerate them.
\emph{Slack identities beat inequalities}: Theorem~\ref{thm:slack} is an equality
with two nonnegative summands, and so delivers the inequality, its rigidity, and
its stability at once. \emph{Dualising a resource constraint}: reading
$k\rho^2 \le 1$ as a function of $\rho$ turns ``the dial is fading'' into ``the
number of independent signals sustainable at the dial level is growing'', and
makes ``floor'' and ``bounded capacity'' literally the same hypothesis.

The theory is deterministic and geometric: it constrains what reported numbers
\emph{can} be given structural facts, not their sampling distribution. The
confidence interval is an input, not an output; the window assumptions
(spread $0.082$, ratios in $[1,1.21]$) are directly measurable and are the
natural targets for falsification. Finally, the pooled quantity here is a
Pearson-type correlation of concatenated blocks; pooling ranks across blocks is a
different operation and is not covered.

\section{Future work}

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Non-uniform block lengths.} Blocks of differing dimension change
the energy weights but not the structure of the argument; the sharp constant
should survive verbatim.
\item \textbf{Rank pooling.} Determine the analogue of the sharp imbalance law
when ranks are recomputed on the concatenation; combinatorial constraints on the
ratio profile should improve $\kappa$.
\item \textbf{Multi-window stability.} A converse to Theorem~\ref{thm:stab} ---
every profile at weighted $L^1$ distance $d$ from the extremiser has slack at
least $cd$ --- would make the slack a two-sided metric on profiles.
\item \textbf{Capacity for correlated families.} Define $\cpc_\gamma(\rho)$ from
$k\rho^2 \le 1 + (k-1)\gamma$ and prove the corresponding duality, so the floor
question can be attacked with weakly correlated families.
\item \textbf{Deciding the dichotomy.} The floor question is now the single
question of whether the ladder's capacity is bounded. Two more rungs at the
observed rate $0.98$ would put the reading below $1/\sqrt{6} \approx 0.4082$ and
the capacity at $6$, refuting the corresponding ceiling.
\end{enumerate}

